    "body": "EICMA 2026 • Yamaha Births New Adventure Scooter Family, Three RW Models That Look More Like Motorcycles\nBentley x FRGMT Torcal Is a New Limited Edition Model\n9 photos\nPhoto: Bentley\nIt’s been almost two months since Bentley pulled the wraps off its first-ever production electric vehicle, the Torcal , and the model has now stepped under the spotlight again with a new limited edition creation.\nThe British luxury automaker calls it “ the first bespoke commission for the Torcal, ” but that’s not exactly what this thing is, because it’s not a one-off creation. In fact, production is capped at 20 units for worldwide markets, and this makes it a limited edition model.\nIt does not have an official name, but the company refers to it as the Bentley x FRGMT Torcal, and it was made in collaboration with Hiroshi Fujiwara. The Bentley ambassador, who is “ a pioneer of Tokyo’s Ura-Harajuku streetwear movement, ” has collaborated with the automaker’s personalization division, Mulliner, in order to create this very special (looking) Torcal .\nAll units of the electric car are presented in the same Beluga solid paint, with a few interesting touches, such as the FRGMT branding on the front fenders and at the rear. These are joined by a set of 22-inch Vortex wheels, which have a black look and Anthracite Satin accents on them.\nPhoto: Bentley Additional highlights can be seen on the inside, where the special Bentley Torcal benefits from a combination of hides in Imperial Blue and Beluga. These are complemented by hand cross-stitching signed by the automaker’s artisans, as well as piano black veneers. Users are greeted by the “Bentley x FRGMT” graphics on the treadplates, which pretty much round off the makeover.\nBentley hasn’t said how many units of the new Torcal FRGMT are still available, nor how much this thing costs, but you can expect to pay more than the regular Torcal, which starts from over $220,000 in the United States of America. The Torcal S kicks off at more than $260,000, and for the First Edition, interested parties will have to pay in excess of $300,000.\nFull production of the Bentley Torcal does not seem to have kicked off at the Crewe factory in the United Kingdom, and that’s not exactly a surprise, given that deliveries are expected to commence in 2027. The model is a zero-emission crossover that shares many nuts and bolts with the Porsche Cayenne Electric, as well as the smaller Macan Electric, and the Audi A6 e-tron, and Q6 e-tron, as all of them are based on the PPE (Premium Platform Electric) construction.\nThe standard Torcal has 810 hp (604 kW/821 ps) and 881 lb-ft (1,194 Nm) of torque combined, hitting 60 mph (97 kph) in 3.3 seconds, and topping out at 155 mph (250 kph). The Torcal S comes with up to 876 hp (888 ps/653 kW) and 996 lb-ft (1,350 Nm) of torque with Launch Control, drops the sprint time to 2.8 seconds, and increases the top speed to 162 mph (260 kph).\nLiked the article? Follow us to see more stories from autoevolution in your Google feeds.\nbentley torcal Bentley Torcal new cars electric vehicles crossover\nRead Press Release\nAbout the author: Cristian Gnaticov\nAfter a series of unfortunate events put an end to Cristian's dream of entering a custom built & tuned old-school Dacia into a rally competition, he moved on to drive press cars and write for a living. He's worked for several automotive online journals and now he's back at autoevolution after his first tour in the mid-2000s. Full profile\nUP NEXT Tesla Wants To Build 1 Million Cybercab Vehicles a Year in Germany",
